1
00:01:24,001 --> 00:01:26,621
<i>Born on a mountain top in Tennessee</i>

2
00:01:27,545 --> 00:01:30,381
<i>Greenest state in the land of the free</i>

3
00:01:30,924 --> 00:01:33,795
<i>Raised in the woods so he knew every tree</i>

4
00:01:34,427 --> 00:01:37,178
<i>Killed him a bar when he was only 3</i>

5
00:01:37,847 --> 00:01:42,539
<i>Davy, Davy Crockett
king of the wild frontier!</i>

6
00:01:48,106 --> 00:01:51,108
<i>Fought single-handed
through the Injun War</i>

7
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:54,644
<i>Till the Creeks was whipped
and peace was in store</i>

8
00:01:55,071 --> 00:01:56,186
<i>And while he was handling...</i>

9
00:01:57,490 --> 00:01:58,487
What'd the doctor say?

10
00:01:58,617 --> 00:02:02,660
Nothing. Supposedly it's just
a 24-hour bug. He gave me some pills.

11
00:02:02,786 --> 00:02:05,076
I told you
you probably just ate some bad gristle.

12
00:02:05,207 --> 00:02:06,785
Should we take the short cut
or the scenic route?

13
00:02:06,917 --> 00:02:08,196
Let's take the short cut.

14
00:02:08,334 --> 00:02:09,911
But the scenic route is so much prettier.

15
00:02:10,043 --> 00:02:12,332
- Okay, let's take the scenic route.
- Great.

16
00:02:12,462 --> 00:02:13,921
It's actually slightly quicker anyway.

17
00:02:24,308 --> 00:02:25,635
Thank you.

18
00:02:34,525 --> 00:02:36,149
What is a squab?

19
00:02:36,276 --> 00:02:39,064
You know what it is.
It's like a pigeon I suppose.

20
00:02:39,196 --> 00:02:40,775
Should we go through the hole
under the horse fence,

21
00:02:40,907 --> 00:02:42,449
or climb the rail over the bridle path?

22
00:02:42,575 --> 00:02:44,697
The horse fence is a little safer.

23
00:02:44,827 --> 00:02:47,946
But the bridle path puts us
right next to the squab shack.

24
00:02:48,331 --> 00:02:50,286
Well, okay.

25
00:02:51,165 --> 00:02:53,158
What's wrong? You're acting skittish.

26
00:02:53,836 --> 00:02:57,169
Don't worry. I've been stealing birds
for a living since before I could trot.

27
00:02:57,298 --> 00:03:01,460
You look unbelievably beautiful tonight.
You're practically glowing.

28
00:03:01,592 --> 00:03:03,171
Maybe it's the lighting.

29
00:03:04,721 --> 00:03:05,800
Come on.

30
00:03:06,555 --> 00:03:11,248
<i>I've been taken for lost and gone
And unknown for a long long time</i>

31
00:03:13,563 --> 00:03:16,978
<i>Fell in love years ago
with an innocent girl</i>

32
00:03:17,108 --> 00:03:21,799
<i>From the Spanish and Indian home
Home of the heroes and villains</i>

33
00:03:27,325 --> 00:03:32,018
<i>And she was right in the rain of the bullets
that eventually brought her down</i>

34
00:03:34,541 --> 00:03:36,913
<i>But she's still dancing in the night</i>

35
00:03:37,043 --> 00:03:41,734
<i>Unafraid of what a dude'll do
in a town full of heroes and villains</i>

36
00:03:43,175 --> 00:03:44,456
What's that?

37
00:03:44,593 --> 00:03:47,001
- I think it's a fox trap. Look at this.
Get away from there.

38
00:03:47,137 --> 00:03:48,250
Is it spring-loaded?

39
00:03:48,387 --> 00:03:52,883
I guess if you come from over there
and stand at this door to the squab shack,

40
00:03:53,017 --> 00:03:54,641
this little gadget probably triggers...

41
00:03:54,768 --> 00:03:56,642
Move out of the way,
that's where it's going to land.

42
00:03:56,771 --> 00:03:58,180
Don't... Let's go!

43
00:04:03,027 --> 00:04:05,315
No, it just falls straight right down.

44
00:04:06,697 --> 00:04:08,071
It's not spring-loaded.

45
00:04:08,197 --> 00:04:09,312
I'm pregnant.

46
00:04:11,909 --> 00:04:14,744
Wow. We're going to have a cub.

47
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:17,194
That's great news.

48
00:04:17,331 --> 00:04:18,956
If we're still alive...

49
00:04:22,753 --> 00:04:27,446
If we're still alive tomorrow morning,
I want you to find another line of work.

50
00:04:28,924 --> 00:04:30,123
Okay.

51
00:04:32,429 --> 00:04:33,840
<i>My children were raised</i>

52
00:04:33,974 --> 00:04:38,350
<i>You know they suddenly rise
They started so long ago</i>

53
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:42,723
<i>Head to toe healthy, wealthy and wise</i>

54
00:04:56,201 --> 00:04:59,321
Does anybody actually read my column?
Do your friends talk about it?

55
00:04:59,455 --> 00:05:02,869
Of course. In fact,
Rabbit's ex-girlfriend said to me,

56
00:05:02,999 --> 00:05:05,573
<i>"I should read Foxy's column. "
But they don't get the Gazette.</i>

57
00:05:05,710 --> 00:05:07,122
Ash! Let's get cracking.

58
00:05:07,254 --> 00:05:08,832
Why would they? It's a rag sheet.

59
00:05:08,964 --> 00:05:10,208
- I'm sick.
- You're not.

60
00:05:10,339 --> 00:05:12,132
- I have a temperature.
- You don't.

61
00:05:12,259 --> 00:05:14,666
- I don't want to go.
- Hurry up. You'll be late.

62
00:05:14,803 --> 00:05:16,760
I love the way you handled that.

63
00:05:21,641 --> 00:05:23,932
Your cousin Kristofferson's
coming on the 6th.

64
00:05:24,062 --> 00:05:27,894
Be extra nice to him,
he's going through a hard time right now.

65
00:05:28,024 --> 00:05:30,348
- Where will he sleep?
- In your room.

66
00:05:30,485 --> 00:05:32,642
I can't spare the space.
Put him in Dad's study.

67
00:05:32,778 --> 00:05:35,400
Dad's study is occupied by Dad.

68
00:05:38,241 --> 00:05:41,576
I don't want to live in a hole anymore.
It makes me feel poor.

69
00:05:41,704 --> 00:05:44,408
We are poor. But we're happy.

70
00:05:44,539 --> 00:05:48,287
<i>Comme ci, comme ça. Anyway,
the views are better above ground.</i>

71
00:05:50,503 --> 00:05:53,954
Honey, I'm 7 non-fox-years old.
My father died at 7-1/2.

72
00:05:54,089 --> 00:05:58,217
I don't want to live in a hole anymore.
I'm going to do something about it.

73
00:06:06,393 --> 00:06:09,559
Well, I'm off. Have a good day, my darlings.

74
00:06:10,479 --> 00:06:13,897
You know, foxes live in holes for a reason.

75
00:06:15,611 --> 00:06:17,021
Yes and no.

76
00:06:20,406 --> 00:06:24,356
What are you wearing? Why a cape
with the pants tucked in your socks?

77
00:06:28,874 --> 00:06:30,665
I guess he's just

78
00:06:32,043 --> 00:06:33,038
different.

79
00:06:36,004 --> 00:06:37,166
I'm not the listing agent on it,

80
00:06:37,298 --> 00:06:40,168
so it doesn't matter to me. I know he's...

81
00:06:40,467 --> 00:06:44,131
Actually, there he is. Mr. Fox! Here it is!

82
00:06:44,261 --> 00:06:48,011
This is the tree! Come on over.
I'll call you back, Bob.

83
00:06:49,267 --> 00:06:52,434
Obviously, it's first growth, indigenous,
original dirt floor.

84
00:06:52,562 --> 00:06:55,266
Good bark, skipping stone hearth,
as you can see.

85
00:06:56,524 --> 00:06:57,982
Kylie. Kylie!

86
00:06:58,108 --> 00:07:00,563
I'm showing the property.
You're not supposed to be here.

87
00:07:00,694 --> 00:07:01,975
What time is it? Sorry.

88
00:07:02,115 --> 00:07:05,150
This is Kylie, the super. He's a little...

89
00:07:05,283 --> 00:07:07,359
What's in the bucket, Mr. Kylie?

90
00:07:07,493 --> 00:07:10,328
See what his eyes look like?
Hey, Kylie!

91
00:07:10,454 --> 00:07:13,325
Huh? Just minnows. Want to try one?

92
00:07:13,457 --> 00:07:15,034
Certainly. Thank you.

93
00:07:17,294 --> 00:07:21,707
It's not exactly an evergreen, is it?
Are there any pines on the market?

94
00:07:21,841 --> 00:07:24,627
Pines are hard to come by
in your price range.

95
00:07:24,760 --> 00:07:26,040
What's that?

96
00:07:36,855 --> 00:07:39,393
What do you do for a living, Mr. Fox?

97
00:07:39,524 --> 00:07:42,358
I used to steal birds,
but now I'm a newspaperman.

98
00:07:42,484 --> 00:07:44,809
Oh, sure. I've seen your byline.

99
00:07:46,781 --> 00:07:48,902
- Good afternoon, gentlemen.
- You're going?

100
00:07:49,031 --> 00:07:53,411
Oh, and Kylie, thank you for the minnow.
It was superb.

101
00:08:02,003 --> 00:08:03,663
Don't buy this tree, Foxy.

102
00:08:03,796 --> 00:08:05,955
You're borrowing at 9.5%
with no fixed rate,

103
00:08:06,090 --> 00:08:08,417
plus moving into the most dangerous
neighborhood in the country

104
00:08:08,552 --> 00:08:10,129
for your species.

105
00:08:10,261 --> 00:08:11,720
You're exaggerating.

106
00:08:13,013 --> 00:08:16,346
I'm sugar-coating it.
This is Boggis, Bunce and Bean,

107
00:08:16,476 --> 00:08:20,258
3 of the meanest, nastiest, ugliest farmers
in the history of this valley.

108
00:08:20,395 --> 00:08:22,102
Really? Tell me about them.

109
00:08:23,898 --> 00:08:24,978
All right.

110
00:08:25,400 --> 00:08:28,851
<i>Walt Boggis is a chicken farmer.
Probably the most successful in the world.</i>

111
00:08:28,988 --> 00:08:31,561
<i>He weighs the same as a young rhinoceros.</i>

112
00:08:31,698 --> 00:08:35,742
<i>He eats 3 chickens every day for
breakfast, lunch, supper and dessert.</i>

113
00:08:35,868 --> 00:08:37,907
<i>That's 12 in total per diem.</i>

114
00:08:38,037 --> 00:08:40,076
<i>Nate Bunce is a duck and goose farmer.</i>

115
00:08:40,205 --> 00:08:42,745
<i>He's approximately the size
of a pot-bellied dwarf,</i>

116
00:08:42,876 --> 00:08:44,121
<i>and his chin would be underwater</i>

117
00:08:44,252 --> 00:08:46,707
<i>in the shallow end of any swimming pool
on the planet.</i>

118
00:08:46,837 --> 00:08:48,414
<i>His food is homemade donuts</i>

119
00:08:48,547 --> 00:08:51,217
<i>with smashed-up goose livers
injected into them.</i>

120
00:08:51,467 --> 00:08:53,958
<i>Frank Bean is a turkey and apple farmer.</i>

121
00:08:54,094 --> 00:08:56,253
<i>He invented his own species of each.</i>

122
00:08:56,555 --> 00:09:01,249
<i>He lives on a liquid diet of strong alcoholic
cider, which he makes from his apples.</i>

123
00:09:02,978 --> 00:09:05,184
<i>He's skinny as a pencil, smart as a whip,</i>

124
00:09:05,314 --> 00:09:08,065
<i>and possibly the scariest man
currently living.</i>

125
00:09:10,650 --> 00:09:15,278
The local human children sing
a kind of eerie little rhyme about him.

126
00:09:15,406 --> 00:09:17,233
Here, listen to this.

127
00:09:17,366 --> 00:09:18,944
<i>Boggis, Bunce and Bean</i>

128
00:09:19,076 --> 00:09:21,283
<i>One fat, one short, one lean</i>

129
00:09:21,412 --> 00:09:23,404
<i>These horrible crooks
So different in looks</i>

130
00:09:23,540 --> 00:09:25,614
<i>Were nonetheless equally mean</i>

131
00:09:26,707 --> 00:09:30,410
In summation, I think
you just got to not do it. That's all.

132
00:09:30,923 --> 00:09:34,372
I understand what you're saying,
and your comments are valuable,

133
00:09:34,507 --> 00:09:36,963
but I'm going to ignore your advice.

134
00:09:37,177 --> 00:09:38,636
The cuss you are.

135
00:09:40,471 --> 00:09:43,390
The cuss am I? Are you cussing with me?

136
00:09:43,517 --> 00:09:45,389
- Are you cussing with me?
- Don't cussing point at me.

137
00:09:45,517 --> 00:09:48,934
Don't cuss with someone
you're not going to cuss with.

138
00:09:57,655 --> 00:09:59,776
- Just buy the tree.
- Okay.

139
00:10:00,239 --> 00:10:03,407
Take a left, then to the right.
Set them down. Help that other guy.

140
00:10:03,535 --> 00:10:06,951
Lift with your legs, not your back.
Don't try to be a superman.

141
00:10:07,248 --> 00:10:11,031
We got 2 circuits here, yellow and green.
Keep them separated.

142
00:10:11,167 --> 00:10:14,203
We need to bring about 2% more in.

143
00:10:14,337 --> 00:10:16,874
A little bit more, a little bit more.
Looks good.

144
00:10:17,214 --> 00:10:20,467
Get that bottom structure settled in.
Bring in the side unit.

145
00:10:20,595 --> 00:10:24,176
Be careful of the branches, guys.
Don't peel away the bark.

146
00:10:28,893 --> 00:10:29,924
Wow.

147
00:10:31,228 --> 00:10:32,426
Hi.

148
00:10:54,250 --> 00:10:56,409
He's slightly younger,
but a cuss of a lot bigger.

149
00:10:56,544 --> 00:11:00,163
That's just genetics, I guess.
Ash has a littler body type.

150
00:11:06,218 --> 00:11:08,258
Go.

151
00:11:09,138 --> 00:11:10,339
Watch this, Dad!

152
00:11:12,686 --> 00:11:14,059
Well, well.

153
00:11:14,644 --> 00:11:16,851
Good jump, Ash.
Remember to keep your tail tucked.

154
00:11:20,400 --> 00:11:22,642
Still painting thunderstorms, I see.

155
00:11:22,777 --> 00:11:24,604
Do you still feel poor?

156
00:11:26,282 --> 00:11:27,480
Less so.

157
00:11:40,126 --> 00:11:43,211
Look at that! This kid's a natural.
I'm speechless, Kristofferson.

158
00:11:51,888 --> 00:11:53,679
Plus, he knows karate.

159
00:11:59,437 --> 00:12:02,143
- Do you think I'm an athlete?
- What are you talking about?

160
00:12:02,275 --> 00:12:04,230
Well, I think I'm an athlete.

161
00:12:04,358 --> 00:12:07,561
Sometimes I feel like you guys
don't see me that way.

162
00:12:07,695 --> 00:12:10,399
What's the subtext here? Is he praying?

163
00:12:10,782 --> 00:12:12,524
I think that's yoga.

164
00:12:15,746 --> 00:12:18,034
How long is Kristofferson
supposed to stay with us?

165
00:12:18,164 --> 00:12:19,706
Until your uncle gets better.

166
00:12:19,832 --> 00:12:22,323
But roughly how long do we plan
to give him on that?

167
00:12:22,459 --> 00:12:25,495
Double pneumonia. It's not that big a deal.

168
00:12:25,920 --> 00:12:27,498
Lower your voice, Ash.

169
00:12:36,806 --> 00:12:38,383
Who am I, Kylie?

170
00:12:38,974 --> 00:12:40,517
Who how? What now?

171
00:12:40,643 --> 00:12:44,510
Why a fox? Why not a horse,
a beetle or a bald eagle?

172
00:12:44,646 --> 00:12:47,599
I'm saying this more as existentialism.

173
00:12:47,732 --> 00:12:50,272
Who am I,
and how can a fox ever be happy

174
00:12:50,403 --> 00:12:53,853
without, forgive the expression,
a chicken in its teeth?

175
00:12:54,656 --> 00:12:57,194
I don't know what you're talking about,
but it sounds illegal.

176
00:12:57,325 --> 00:12:59,402
Here, put this bandit hat on.

177
00:13:00,579 --> 00:13:03,449
Maybe you're a medium. Take it off
and don't wear it around the house.

178
00:13:05,250 --> 00:13:06,793
And so it begins.

179
00:13:09,254 --> 00:13:12,124
Do you mind if I slide my bedroll
slightly out from under the train set?

180
00:13:12,256 --> 00:13:14,711
It's hard to sleep
in that corkscrew position.

181
00:13:14,841 --> 00:13:19,533
There's a lot of attitudes going on
around here. Don't let me get one.

182
00:13:20,305 --> 00:13:21,883
It's just that my spinal cord is...

183
00:13:22,015 --> 00:13:23,178
Sleep wherever you want.

184
00:13:23,309 --> 00:13:27,222
Here, take my bed.
I'll crawl under the bookcase.

185
00:13:27,353 --> 00:13:29,560
Who cares if I get splinters in my ears?

186
00:13:29,689 --> 00:13:31,349
- Never mind.
- Are you going to pout about it?

187
00:13:31,483 --> 00:13:34,981
I've had it up to here
with the sad house guest routine.

188
00:13:42,785 --> 00:13:44,066
Good night.

189
00:14:41,299 --> 00:14:44,419
I used to do this professionally
and I was very successful at it.

190
00:14:44,554 --> 00:14:45,833
I had to get out of it for personal reasons,

191
00:14:45,972 --> 00:14:49,884
but I've decided to secretly do
one last big job on the sly.

192
00:14:50,015 --> 00:14:52,850
I'm bringing you in as my secretary
and personal assistant.

193
00:14:52,976 --> 00:14:54,055
Okay.

194
00:14:54,728 --> 00:14:57,645
This is actually kind of a big deal,
so don't just say, "Okay. "

195
00:14:57,772 --> 00:14:58,803
Okay, thank you.

196
00:14:58,939 --> 00:15:02,356
I'm going to tape this for my records,
so don't make a lot of sounds.

197
00:15:02,486 --> 00:15:04,313
Meaning, stop rocking.

198
00:15:05,405 --> 00:15:07,314
Master Plan. Phase one. Side A.

199
00:15:07,907 --> 00:15:09,984
We'll start with
Boggis' Chicken House Number One.

200
00:15:10,118 --> 00:15:13,321
His only security is a few old
hunting beagles and a low stone wall.

201
00:15:13,454 --> 00:15:16,988
A word about beagles,
never look a beagle directly in the eye.

202
00:15:17,248 --> 00:15:19,741
Why not? Beagles aren't so tough.

203
00:15:20,462 --> 00:15:24,506
One of these beagles has chronic rabies,
which he's on medication for.

204
00:15:24,632 --> 00:15:28,297
If you get bitten by him, you have to get
shots in your stomach for 6 months.

205
00:15:28,427 --> 00:15:29,672
I'm not going to justify this.

206
00:15:29,804 --> 00:15:32,473
Just pay attention and stop interrupting.
I'm taping this.

207
00:15:32,597 --> 00:15:34,508
I picked some blueberries and laced each

208
00:15:34,642 --> 00:15:36,801
with 10 mg of high potency
sleeping powder.

209
00:15:36,936 --> 00:15:38,181
Enough to tranquilize a gorilla.

210
00:15:38,561 --> 00:15:41,349
- How do we make them eat it?
- Beagles love blueberries.

211
00:15:41,481 --> 00:15:44,648
<i>Remember, they aren't very
smart, but they're incredibly paranoid,</i>

212
00:15:44,776 --> 00:15:46,981
<i>so always kill a chicken in one bite.</i>

213
00:15:47,695 --> 00:15:49,272
One bite, get it?

214
00:15:51,073 --> 00:15:52,698
Are you listening to me?
I look in your eyes

215
00:15:52,825 --> 00:15:54,533
and can't tell if you get anything I say.

216
00:15:59,206 --> 00:16:00,487
Magnesium.

217
00:16:01,750 --> 00:16:03,374
- Magnesium!
- Sorry.

218
00:16:05,294 --> 00:16:06,326
Pipette.

219
00:16:06,463 --> 00:16:08,172
- Pipette!
- Oh, sorry.

220
00:16:09,092 --> 00:16:11,463
Potassium... What are you looking at?

221
00:16:11,593 --> 00:16:12,755
Oh, no.

222
00:16:14,887 --> 00:16:16,964
Why's your cousin such a wet sandwich?

223
00:16:17,098 --> 00:16:18,640
- I beg your pardon?
- What's that mean?

224
00:16:18,767 --> 00:16:20,889
That means I didn't understand,
a wet sandwich?

225
00:16:21,018 --> 00:16:24,389
A wet sandwich, he's too short,
he dresses like a girl, he's

226
00:16:24,773 --> 00:16:25,769
different.

227
00:16:25,899 --> 00:16:28,686
Are you a bully?
You're starting to sound like a bully.

228
00:16:29,234 --> 00:16:30,398
Watch this.

229
00:16:32,405 --> 00:16:34,029
You just destroyed the whole experiment.

230
00:16:34,156 --> 00:16:36,777
We better extinguish this magnesium.
Stand back.

231
00:16:43,707 --> 00:16:45,285
I like your ears.

232
00:16:45,583 --> 00:16:46,662
Mine?

233
00:16:46,793 --> 00:16:49,580
Mmm-hmm.
- Thank you. I like your spots.

234
00:16:49,713 --> 00:16:52,914
Really? I used to cover them up,
but, you know...

235
00:16:54,383 --> 00:16:57,338
- You're supposed to be my lab partner.
- I am.

236
00:16:57,929 --> 00:17:00,218
No, you're not. You're disloyal.

237
00:17:21,493 --> 00:17:24,363
A few beagles, as we discussed,
but we're ready for that.

238
00:17:25,579 --> 00:17:29,446
In the old days, didn't we do a thing
when somebody saw a wolf...

239
00:17:29,584 --> 00:17:31,161
Wolf? What wolf?

240
00:17:33,629 --> 00:17:35,289
Nothing? Never mind.

241
00:17:36,590 --> 00:17:39,081
Here comes a little stone wall.
Not a problem.

242
00:17:43,180 --> 00:17:46,098
What the cuss? Where did this giant fence
come from? We had a master plan.

243
00:17:46,226 --> 00:17:47,257
What's this lightning bolt?

244
00:17:48,728 --> 00:17:51,811
It could mean the fence might be electric.

245
00:17:51,938 --> 00:17:55,223
I hope it doesn't mean thunder,
because I have a phobia of that.

246
00:17:55,358 --> 00:17:56,603
Ah!

247
00:18:05,453 --> 00:18:06,779
Watch this.

248
00:18:30,892 --> 00:18:34,176
Beagles love blueberries. Didn't I tell you?
The master plan is working again.

249
00:18:35,354 --> 00:18:36,815
This is the tricky part.

250
00:18:36,941 --> 00:18:38,683
One of us has to jump the barbed wire,

251
00:18:38,818 --> 00:18:41,354
slide under the tire spikes
and open the fence latch.

252
00:18:41,485 --> 00:18:43,193
- Who will it be?
- Not me.

253
00:18:43,321 --> 00:18:46,820
Kristofferson could do this easily.
He's like an Olympic level...

254
00:18:46,949 --> 00:18:49,357
Why don't we run that way?
There's no obstacles.

255
00:18:50,869 --> 00:18:52,032
Yeah, that's better.

256
00:19:02,714 --> 00:19:04,125
- I said, one bite.
- I'm trying!

257
00:19:04,257 --> 00:19:07,043
I have a different kind of teeth from you.
I'm an opossum.

258
00:19:07,510 --> 00:19:08,541
Give me that.

259
00:19:10,888 --> 00:19:12,549
That's so grisly. There's blood.

260
00:19:12,682 --> 00:19:13,880
Follow me.

261
00:19:30,032 --> 00:19:32,238
All right, what's the master escape plan?

262
00:19:35,454 --> 00:19:36,651
Follow me again.

263
00:19:42,920 --> 00:19:43,916
Quick!

264
00:19:45,963 --> 00:19:47,244
Give me that!

265
00:19:58,141 --> 00:20:00,300
Let's hit the 5-and-dime
on the way home.

266
00:20:00,435 --> 00:20:02,926
We'll make fake price tags
and wrap the chickens in wax paper,

267
00:20:03,063 --> 00:20:04,889
so it looks like we bought them!

268
00:20:11,486 --> 00:20:12,565
Huh!

269
00:20:14,615 --> 00:20:16,111
Where'd you get this chicken?

270
00:20:16,243 --> 00:20:18,699
At the 5-and-dime last night.

271
00:20:18,829 --> 00:20:21,614
It has a Boggis Farms tag around its ankle.

272
00:20:21,748 --> 00:20:25,033
Huh! Must have escaped from there
before I bought it.

273
00:20:31,507 --> 00:20:32,503
Psst!

274
00:20:32,968 --> 00:20:35,802
Bunce tonight. He has a refrigerated
smokehouse with geese...

275
00:20:35,928 --> 00:20:39,794
I thought you said
we were only doing one last big job.

276
00:20:39,931 --> 00:20:43,383
We are. But it's not done yet.
It's a triple-header.

277
00:21:15,549 --> 00:21:16,663
Shh! Shh!

278
00:21:20,803 --> 00:21:21,800
Huh.

279
00:21:22,306 --> 00:21:23,634
Let's see some hustle.

280
00:21:23,766 --> 00:21:26,089
I've never played whack-bat.
What are the rules?

281
00:21:26,226 --> 00:21:28,264
No whack-bat on the other side
of the river?

282
00:21:28,393 --> 00:21:31,679
No, we mostly just run grass sprints
or play acorns.

283
00:21:32,231 --> 00:21:33,725
It's real simple.

284
00:21:33,857 --> 00:21:37,523
<i>There's 3 grabbers, 3 taggers,
5 twig-runners and the player at whack-bat.</i>

285
00:21:37,653 --> 00:21:40,025
<i>The center-tagger lights a pinecone
and chucks it over the basket.</i>

286
00:21:40,155 --> 00:21:42,693
<i>The whack-batter tries to hit
the cedar-stick off the cross-rock.</i>

287
00:21:42,825 --> 00:21:44,318
<i>The twig-runners dash back and forth</i>

288
00:21:44,451 --> 00:21:46,739
<i>until the pinecone burns out
and the umpire calls, "hot box. "</i>

289
00:21:47,120 --> 00:21:49,527
At the end, you count
how many score-downs it adds up to

290
00:21:49,665 --> 00:21:50,658
and divide by 9.

291
00:21:50,789 --> 00:21:52,165
- Got it.
- Go in for Ash.

292
00:21:52,291 --> 00:21:55,542
Substitution! Ash, come out!
You need a breather.

293
00:21:55,670 --> 00:21:57,130
Come out?

294
00:21:57,256 --> 00:22:00,339
What? I still feel good, Coach.
Let me finish this 8th.

295
00:22:00,466 --> 00:22:03,383
No, come on. Step out, let's go.

296
00:22:05,762 --> 00:22:09,595
- Am I getting better, Coach?
- You're sure as cuss not getting worse.

297
00:22:09,725 --> 00:22:13,640
You think I could end up being as good
as my dad if I keep practicing?

298
00:22:13,771 --> 00:22:17,388
Your dad? Your dad was probably
the best whack-bat player

299
00:22:17,524 --> 00:22:19,397
we ever had in this school.

300
00:22:24,196 --> 00:22:26,568
Don't compare yourself to that.

301
00:22:26,698 --> 00:22:30,946
But I think I have some of the same
raw natural talent, don't you?

302
00:22:31,328 --> 00:22:34,080
You're improving, let's put it like that.

303
00:22:59,648 --> 00:23:00,645
Hot box!

304
00:23:00,775 --> 00:23:02,317
Divide that by 9, please!

305
00:23:03,902 --> 00:23:06,606
That's the first time
this kid's ever swung a whack bat?

306
00:23:06,738 --> 00:23:08,979
He really is your father's nephew.

307
00:23:09,115 --> 00:23:10,657
- Not by blood.
- No?

308
00:23:10,783 --> 00:23:13,320
- He's from my mother's side.
- Oh, yeah.

309
00:23:14,744 --> 00:23:16,536
What's that stand for?

310
00:23:17,497 --> 00:23:19,869
It's for pep. Pep.

311
00:23:19,999 --> 00:23:20,995
It's a K.

312
00:23:21,125 --> 00:23:23,581
Come on now, look alive! That-a-boy!

313
00:23:24,463 --> 00:23:25,625
We're going steady.

314
00:23:30,509 --> 00:23:31,505
What's that?

315
00:23:31,635 --> 00:23:33,428
What? What, this?

316
00:23:33,931 --> 00:23:37,216
Nothing. Just some old trophy I won
for being an athlete.

317
00:23:38,893 --> 00:23:41,301
I have to cover a book party
at some animals nest

318
00:23:41,437 --> 00:23:42,896
in a tobacco field down the hill.

319
00:23:43,022 --> 00:23:46,556
Kylie and I are going to give it a whirl.
Don't wait up.

320
00:23:46,692 --> 00:23:48,234
What's the book?

321
00:23:48,485 --> 00:23:51,357
Some memoir.
I'll get him to sign you a copy.

322
00:23:51,489 --> 00:23:53,696
The dinner was pitch perfect.

323
00:24:01,206 --> 00:24:05,155
I saw a couple broken burglar bars under
the back door to Bean's secret cider cellar.

324
00:24:05,292 --> 00:24:07,166
- We're breaking into Bean's house?
- Cellar.

325
00:24:07,295 --> 00:24:09,122
- Where he lives?
- Where he keeps the cider.

326
00:24:09,255 --> 00:24:10,665
Below where he lives.

327
00:24:10,798 --> 00:24:12,755
Where'd you come from? Go back
to the tree and do your homework.

328
00:24:12,883 --> 00:24:14,259
I want to help steal cider.

329
00:24:14,385 --> 00:24:17,006
We're going to a book party.
And be quiet about any cider,

330
00:24:17,137 --> 00:24:19,176
because nobody said that. Now, go!

331
00:24:19,305 --> 00:24:21,677
You're going to get me in a lot of trouble.

332
00:24:21,808 --> 00:24:23,932
Besides, you're too little
and uncoordinated.

333
00:24:26,606 --> 00:24:27,885
1, 2, 3!

334
00:24:29,942 --> 00:24:31,685
Where the cuss does that kid get off?

335
00:24:31,818 --> 00:24:33,692
Can you believe that?
How'd he get tipped off?

336
00:24:37,157 --> 00:24:38,236
You think he'll tell on us?

337
00:24:40,202 --> 00:24:42,693
Before we go on,
can you give me some kind of signal

338
00:24:42,829 --> 00:24:44,868
so I know this is getting through to you?

339
00:24:47,708 --> 00:24:49,202
That's it? All right.

340
00:24:49,334 --> 00:24:50,711
There's another one.

341
00:24:51,170 --> 00:24:53,838
- Good, you made it. Anybody see you?
- I don't think so.

342
00:24:53,963 --> 00:24:55,587
Put this bandit hat on.

343
00:24:57,592 --> 00:25:00,878
I'm pleased to be invited,
but I'm not sure I should be doing this.

344
00:25:01,012 --> 00:25:03,385
- Why not?
- I don't like to be dishonest with people.

345
00:25:03,515 --> 00:25:05,389
Just keep your mouth shut
and it won't be a problem.

346
00:25:05,518 --> 00:25:07,889
- I don't think he should come, either.
- We're not taking a vote!

347
00:25:11,104 --> 00:25:12,351
One time, this wolf I saw...

348
00:25:12,483 --> 00:25:15,400
What's with all the wolf talk?
Give it a rest for once.

349
00:25:28,163 --> 00:25:30,037
Look at all this apple juice.

350
00:25:30,166 --> 00:25:33,499
Apple juice?
We didn't come here for apple juice.

351
00:25:33,627 --> 00:25:37,410
This is some of the strongest,
finest alcoholic cider money can buy

352
00:25:37,546 --> 00:25:39,457
or that can even be stolen.

353
00:25:39,591 --> 00:25:42,081
It burns in your throat,
boils in your stomach,

354
00:25:42,217 --> 00:25:45,338
and tastes almost exactly
like pure, melted gold.

355
00:25:54,938 --> 00:25:58,686
Y'all are trespassing now, illegally.

356
00:26:04,699 --> 00:26:08,778
'Round these parts,
we don't take kindly to cider poachers.

357
00:26:09,286 --> 00:26:10,660
You've aged badly, Rat.

358
00:26:11,371 --> 00:26:14,954
You're getting a little long in the tooth
yourself, partner.

359
00:26:18,211 --> 00:26:20,999
Why are you wearing that badge?
What is that?

360
00:26:22,590 --> 00:26:24,298
It's my job.

361
00:26:39,356 --> 00:26:41,314
How's your old lady doing?

362
00:26:41,442 --> 00:26:43,315
Do you refer to my wife?

363
00:26:44,443 --> 00:26:46,816
She was the town tart in her day.

364
00:26:46,946 --> 00:26:48,820
Wild and footloose and

365
00:26:49,865 --> 00:26:51,193
pretty as a mink stole.

366
00:26:51,577 --> 00:26:52,656
Is that true?

367
00:26:53,496 --> 00:26:56,697
Of course not. I mean,
certainly she lived. We all did.

368
00:26:56,830 --> 00:26:59,832
It was a different time.
Let's not use a double standard.

369
00:26:59,959 --> 00:27:01,619
- But town tart?
- Shut up.

370
00:27:04,046 --> 00:27:05,790
That was close, Rat. Be careful.

371
00:27:06,382 --> 00:27:08,459
Oh, I'm careful as a...

372
00:27:34,367 --> 00:27:36,775
How many jars should I bring up, Franklin?

373
00:27:36,911 --> 00:27:38,653
I don't know.
2, I guess.

374
00:27:38,786 --> 00:27:40,743
You drank 3 yesterday.

375
00:27:41,124 --> 00:27:42,951
All right. Take 3.

376
00:27:54,178 --> 00:27:55,885
Nope. 2 is plenty.

377
00:28:00,517 --> 00:28:03,222
Oh, my cuss. Is she blind?

378
00:28:03,353 --> 00:28:06,722
She might have astigmatism
or possibly a cataract of some form.

379
00:28:06,856 --> 00:28:08,398
Anyway, her eyes don't see well.

380
00:28:22,788 --> 00:28:25,113
What'd I tell you?
This kid's a natural! Am I right?

381
00:28:30,796 --> 00:28:33,833
So good of you to come.
You both look splendid.

382
00:28:33,966 --> 00:28:36,040
How have you been, Walter?
In good health?

383
00:28:37,009 --> 00:28:38,586
Nathan? AII's well?

384
00:28:38,719 --> 00:28:39,964
Wonderful.

385
00:28:41,597 --> 00:28:43,175
Any fox problems?

386
00:28:43,808 --> 00:28:44,838
- Are you joking?
- Horrible.

387
00:28:44,976 --> 00:28:45,970
- We're miserable.
- He's laughing at us.

388
00:28:46,100 --> 00:28:47,133
- Humiliating.
- We're furious.

389
00:28:47,270 --> 00:28:48,301
I don't want to talk about it.

390
00:28:56,153 --> 00:28:57,694
Perhaps we ought to kill him.

391
00:28:59,072 --> 00:29:01,278
- That seems obvious.
- He's too sneaky.

392
00:29:01,657 --> 00:29:05,027
Right, of course. He's very
clever, isn't he?

393
00:29:05,161 --> 00:29:07,652
Might be a bit difficult, I suppose.

394
00:29:17,214 --> 00:29:20,584
I've figured out where this fox lives.
Tomorrow night, we'll camp in the bushes,

395
00:29:20,718 --> 00:29:24,218
wait for him to come out
and shoot the cuss to smithereens.

396
00:29:24,347 --> 00:29:26,090
How does that grab you?

397
00:29:26,807 --> 00:29:28,467
I don't see why not.

398
00:29:40,071 --> 00:29:41,777
Another book party?

399
00:29:43,614 --> 00:29:46,698
I didn't see you sitting in the dark
over there.

400
00:29:47,577 --> 00:29:50,492
Actually, there was a fire.
I just got the call.

401
00:29:50,619 --> 00:29:53,538
May be arson.
I have to interview the marshal...

402
00:29:53,665 --> 00:29:56,334
Kylie. Is he telling the truth?

403
00:29:57,794 --> 00:29:59,170
I don't want to be put in
the middle of this.

404
00:29:59,295 --> 00:30:00,327
Thanks, Kylie.

405
00:30:00,463 --> 00:30:02,752
Why is he wearing that bandit hat?

406
00:30:05,926 --> 00:30:08,595
His ears were cold. He's not with us.
Go back to bed.

407
00:30:10,390 --> 00:30:13,178
If what I think is happening is happening,

408
00:30:14,060 --> 00:30:15,257
it better not be.

409
00:30:33,078 --> 00:30:34,703
Nice job covering for me. Next time...

410
00:30:49,927 --> 00:30:50,923
All 3!

411
00:30:51,262 --> 00:30:52,293
Kill him!

412
00:31:15,743 --> 00:31:17,903
We got the tail, but we missed the fox.

413
00:31:20,623 --> 00:31:24,074
Petey, sorry to wake you.
Can you dash out here right away

414
00:31:24,210 --> 00:31:28,338
with 3 shovels, 2 pickaxes,
500 rounds of ammunition,

415
00:31:28,963 --> 00:31:30,292
and a bottle of apple cider?

416
00:31:32,049 --> 00:31:34,755
It'll grow back, won't it?
- Tails don't grow back.

417
00:31:34,888 --> 00:31:37,092
- Tails don't grow back?
- Mmm-mmm. Except lizards'.

418
00:31:37,221 --> 00:31:40,922
Tails don't grow back. I'm gonna be
tail-less for the rest of my life.

419
00:31:41,060 --> 00:31:43,764
Anyway, it's not half as bad
as double pneumonia, right?

420
00:31:43,895 --> 00:31:46,930
I mean, his dad has one foot in the grave
and 3 feet on a banana peel.

421
00:31:47,063 --> 00:31:48,855
That's a lot worse than...

422
00:31:51,987 --> 00:31:54,393
Excuse me. I'm going to go meditate
for half an hour.

423
00:31:55,990 --> 00:31:58,991
You have 29 minutes
to come up with a proper apology.

424
00:31:59,118 --> 00:32:01,241
Me? Me have an apology?

425
00:32:01,787 --> 00:32:05,120
He just got here and got a bandit hat?
Where's my bandit hat?

426
00:32:05,248 --> 00:32:09,543
Why didn't I get shot at? Because
you think I'm no good at anything!

427
00:32:09,670 --> 00:32:11,496
Maybe you're right, thanks.

428
00:32:14,215 --> 00:32:15,794
I told you not to bring him.

429
00:32:16,385 --> 00:32:18,459
Why the cuss
didn't I listen to my lawyer?

430
00:32:18,594 --> 00:32:22,758
We'll be lucky to flip this tree
for half of what we've sunk into it.

431
00:32:22,891 --> 00:32:27,268
I can't sleep on my back for 6 weeks
and on my stomach, I feel congested.

432
00:32:27,395 --> 00:32:29,387
Why the cuss didn't I listen to my lawyer?

433
00:32:29,523 --> 00:32:31,514
Because you
don't listen to anybody.

434
00:32:33,276 --> 00:32:34,307
- What was that?
I said...

435
00:32:35,569 --> 00:32:37,111
Wake up! They're digging us out!

436
00:32:37,278 --> 00:32:39,771
- They'll kill the children.
- Over my dead body.

437
00:32:39,908 --> 00:32:43,027
- I know. You're dead, too, in that scenario.
- I'm arguing against that.

438
00:32:43,160 --> 00:32:44,489
- What?
- Why are you yelling?

439
00:32:44,620 --> 00:32:48,948
Stop! You say one thing, she says another,
and it all changes back again!

440
00:32:59,634 --> 00:33:02,338
I've got it. No time to lose.
Why didn't I think of this sooner?

441
00:33:02,469 --> 00:33:03,845
Think of what?

442
00:33:03,971 --> 00:33:05,596
We've been trapped before.

443
00:33:06,015 --> 00:33:08,302
Dig!

444
00:33:28,076 --> 00:33:31,827
I think it's time for me to give a pep talk
and explain some things.

445
00:33:32,582 --> 00:33:33,577
A very long time ago...

446
00:33:33,707 --> 00:33:35,784
May I have a word with you privately?

447
00:33:35,918 --> 00:33:37,496
Well, we're in a hole here.

448
00:33:37,629 --> 00:33:40,913
On the other side of this mineral deposit.
Follow me.

449
00:33:43,927 --> 00:33:46,383
I'm going
to lose my temper now.

450
00:33:47,138 --> 00:33:48,466
- When?
- Right now.

451
00:33:49,264 --> 00:33:50,295
Well, when?

452
00:33:54,477 --> 00:33:57,596
12 fox-years ago,
you made a promise

453
00:33:57,730 --> 00:34:00,600
while we were caged in that fox trap
that, if we survived,

454
00:34:00,732 --> 00:34:03,651
you would never steal another chicken,
turkey, goose, duck

455
00:34:03,779 --> 00:34:05,237
or squab, whatever they are.

456
00:34:05,362 --> 00:34:09,063
Now, I believed you.
Why did you lie to me?

457
00:34:10,868 --> 00:34:12,741
Because I'm a wild animal.

458
00:34:13,120 --> 00:34:15,788
You are also a husband, and a father!

459
00:34:16,872 --> 00:34:18,914
I'm trying to tell you the truth
about myself.

460
00:34:19,586 --> 00:34:22,290
I don't care about the truth about yourself.

461
00:34:22,963 --> 00:34:25,749
This story is too predictable.

462
00:34:26,466 --> 00:34:29,502
Predictable, really?
What happens in the end?

463
00:34:30,303 --> 00:34:32,841
In the end, we all die.

464
00:34:34,098 --> 00:34:35,760
Unless you change.

465
00:34:51,240 --> 00:34:55,237
Petey, run down to the rental department
at Malloy Consolidated and place an order

466
00:34:55,368 --> 00:34:59,283
for one Mighty Max, one Junior Spitfire,

467
00:34:59,415 --> 00:35:03,281
and a long-range Tornado 375 Turbo.
For immediate delivery.

468
00:35:11,843 --> 00:35:12,873
Huh?

469
00:35:13,011 --> 00:35:14,469
- Huh?
- Huh?

470
00:35:23,645 --> 00:35:27,145
<i>Everywhere I hear the sound</i>

471
00:35:27,275 --> 00:35:31,770
<i>of marching charging feet, boy</i>

472
00:35:32,737 --> 00:35:36,486
<i>Cause summer's here and the time is right</i>

473
00:35:36,616 --> 00:35:41,194
<i>For fighting in the streets, boy</i>

474
00:35:42,288 --> 00:35:45,704
Ash, are you mad at me?
I understand if you are, and I'm sorry.

475
00:35:45,833 --> 00:35:48,918
I wouldn't have involved your cousin
if I'd realized you'd feel this way.

476
00:35:49,045 --> 00:35:53,337
It was only ever because he kind of
a natural, I mean... Look at him dig!

477
00:35:56,259 --> 00:35:57,590
Anyway, I'm sorry if...

478
00:35:57,721 --> 00:35:59,879
I'm going to just put dirt in my ears.

479
00:36:00,014 --> 00:36:01,887
That's better. I can't hear you now,
but keep talking.

480
00:36:02,892 --> 00:36:03,841
<i>Get down</i>

481
00:36:24,620 --> 00:36:26,493
I don't have beagle ticks, by the way.

482
00:36:26,621 --> 00:36:31,249
Well, me, neither. Whoever
said we had beagle ticks, by the way?

483
00:36:31,377 --> 00:36:34,709
Apparently, that's what you've been
telling everyone. Beagle ticks and pelt lice.

484
00:36:34,838 --> 00:36:39,464
I never said that. And you're misquoting
me. But I'll get to the bottom of it.

485
00:36:40,344 --> 00:36:42,585
We may or may not ever see
the light of day again,

486
00:36:42,720 --> 00:36:45,293
but I really like Agnes
and I think she likes me.

487
00:36:45,763 --> 00:36:47,724
Great. She's a free agent. What do I care?

488
00:36:47,852 --> 00:36:49,180
Then why are you dead set...

489
00:36:49,311 --> 00:36:51,598
- Can I ask you a question?
- You may.

490
00:36:51,729 --> 00:36:54,434
What's the point of sitting on the floor
with your legs twisted into a pretzel

491
00:36:54,565 --> 00:36:57,352
talking to yourself
for an hour and 45 minutes? It's weird.

492
00:36:57,485 --> 00:37:00,438
My father and I started meditating
together when...

493
00:37:00,570 --> 00:37:03,987
That's great, but I'd worry more about
what that does to your reputation

494
00:37:04,117 --> 00:37:06,405
than whether you have beagle ticks or not.

495
00:37:07,911 --> 00:37:09,904
I don't. Nor pelt lice.

496
00:37:13,500 --> 00:37:18,041
One of those slovenly farmers is probably
wearing my tail as a necktie by now.

497
00:37:18,171 --> 00:37:19,368
You're paranoid, Foxy.

498
00:37:19,798 --> 00:37:22,632
<i>Farmer, correct me
if I'm misreading the data.</i>

499
00:37:22,758 --> 00:37:25,879
<i>You've destroyed the scenery,
but the alleged fox remains at large.</i>

500
00:37:26,012 --> 00:37:27,755
Look at Dad's tie.

501
00:37:27,888 --> 00:37:30,095
<i>What will you 3 prominent farmers
do now?</i>

502
00:37:30,224 --> 00:37:32,595
<i>I can tell you what we're not going to do.</i>

503
00:37:32,725 --> 00:37:34,469
<i>We're not going to let him go.</i>

504
00:37:46,782 --> 00:37:48,940
Stand clear, please. Stand clear, everyone.

505
00:37:49,868 --> 00:37:50,899
Contact!

506
00:38:05,007 --> 00:38:06,880
Boggis, how many men work
on your farm?

507
00:38:07,008 --> 00:38:08,040
-35.
- Bunce?

508
00:38:08,176 --> 00:38:09,256
-36.
- I've got 37.

509
00:38:09,387 --> 00:38:12,173
That... Carry the zero, divide by 2...

510
00:38:12,305 --> 00:38:14,014
That's 108 all together.

511
00:38:15,517 --> 00:38:18,471
Petey, drop everything
and assemble all 108 members

512
00:38:18,603 --> 00:38:20,643
of our 3 combined work forces.

513
00:38:20,772 --> 00:38:23,856
We'll starve them out, then kill them.
Starting in

514
00:38:24,566 --> 00:38:26,644
shall we say, 15 minutes?

515
00:38:28,197 --> 00:38:31,363
An estimated
108 snipers are in position,

516
00:38:31,491 --> 00:38:34,028
surrounding the demolished fox residence.

517
00:38:34,160 --> 00:38:37,077
Any local animals appear to be
trapped underground

518
00:38:37,204 --> 00:38:39,281
without provisions of any kind.

519
00:38:39,415 --> 00:38:43,994
If I had a crystal ball, I'd predict
a fairly grisly outcome to this situation.

520
00:38:44,129 --> 00:38:48,504
We'll stay on the scene, watching closely,
as events continue to unfold.

521
00:38:49,591 --> 00:38:53,671
This is going to be a total cluster-cuss
for everybody.

522
00:39:00,644 --> 00:39:02,849
How long can a fox go
without food or water?

523
00:39:03,688 --> 00:39:07,354
I can only answer as an opossum, but I
can't last more than another couple hours

524
00:39:07,484 --> 00:39:09,773
before I get completely dehydrated
and starve to death.

525
00:39:09,903 --> 00:39:11,065
What's that?

526
00:39:18,745 --> 00:39:20,368
Dad.
Not a sound.

527
00:39:30,005 --> 00:39:31,747
You scared the cuss out of us!

528
00:39:34,384 --> 00:39:38,334
A lot of good animals are probably going
to die, because of you!

529
00:39:41,057 --> 00:39:43,180
We've been digging in circles for 3 days.

530
00:39:43,309 --> 00:39:44,589
Half the woods have been obliterated.

531
00:39:44,726 --> 00:39:48,142
Nobody can get out. My wife's huddled
at the bottom of a flint-mine

532
00:39:48,271 --> 00:39:51,854
with no food, no water,
and 27 starving animal brats!

533
00:39:53,110 --> 00:39:56,478
I just want to see a little sunshine.

534
00:39:56,737 --> 00:40:00,071
You're nocturnal, Phil. Your eyes
barely even open on a good day.

535
00:40:00,200 --> 00:40:02,238
I'm sick of your double-talk!
We have rights!

536
00:40:02,951 --> 00:40:04,743
We don't like you and we hate your dad.

537
00:40:04,872 --> 00:40:07,409
Now grab some mud,
chew it and swallow it.

538
00:40:07,541 --> 00:40:10,030
- I'm not going to eat mud.
- Cuss, yeah, you are.

539
00:40:17,675 --> 00:40:19,085
Don't do that.

540
00:40:19,926 --> 00:40:20,923
Why'd you take your shoes off?

541
00:40:21,053 --> 00:40:23,212
So I don't break your nose when I kick it.

542
00:40:33,398 --> 00:40:35,271
I can fight my own fights.

543
00:40:36,233 --> 00:40:37,396
No, you can't.

544
00:40:38,027 --> 00:40:41,111
Those farmers won't quit till they've
got you and every member of your family

545
00:40:41,238 --> 00:40:43,944
nailed upside-down to a bloody stick
with your eyes gorged out.

546
00:40:46,077 --> 00:40:48,069
This is getting a little too personal.

547
00:40:49,372 --> 00:40:50,782
Give me a minute.

548
00:41:06,178 --> 00:41:07,460
I've got an idea.

549
00:41:07,598 --> 00:41:08,712
- What?
- It could be good.

550
00:41:08,849 --> 00:41:10,473
- Lay it on us.
- It might save our lives.

551
00:41:10,600 --> 00:41:11,976
Say the idea!

552
00:41:12,603 --> 00:41:14,511
All right. Let's try it.

553
00:41:16,021 --> 00:41:19,557
<i>Go to the flint-mine, tell Mrs. Badger et al.
that help is on the way.</i>

554
00:41:19,692 --> 00:41:21,353
Is help on the way?

555
00:41:21,486 --> 00:41:23,312
I sure as cuss hope so.

556
00:41:29,367 --> 00:41:32,903
Ash, I know what it's like to feel

557
00:41:34,372 --> 00:41:35,535
different.

558
00:41:36,833 --> 00:41:38,114
I'm not different.

559
00:41:38,877 --> 00:41:40,156
Am I?

560
00:41:40,544 --> 00:41:42,833
We all are. Him, especially.

561
00:41:43,548 --> 00:41:46,668
But there's something kind of fantastic
about that, isn't there?

562
00:41:52,765 --> 00:41:55,470
Not to me. I'd prefer to be an athlete.

563
00:41:57,687 --> 00:41:59,061
Gentlemen,

564
00:41:59,187 --> 00:42:02,355
this time we must dig
in a very special direction.

565
00:42:07,571 --> 00:42:10,192
We have to kind of feel out the vibe.

566
00:42:13,451 --> 00:42:14,482
Begin.

567
00:42:38,933 --> 00:42:42,883
Whoo-hoo! Come on! You guys...
You're not... Come on.

568
00:42:49,444 --> 00:42:52,232
I hit it slap in the middle.
Do you get how incredible this is?

569
00:42:55,074 --> 00:42:59,238
<i>'Bout a handsome little fox
Let me sing you folks a yarn</i>

570
00:42:59,370 --> 00:43:03,830
<i>Hey, diddle-dee, daddle-da,
doddle-do, doodle-dum</i>

571
00:43:03,957 --> 00:43:07,575
<i>'Twas a splendid little fella
Full of wit and grace and charm</i>

572
00:43:07,712 --> 00:43:11,661
<i>Say zippy-zee, zappy-za,
yappy-yo, goggle-gum</i>

573
00:43:11,798 --> 00:43:16,491
<i>Like any little critter needing
Vittles for his little-uns</i>

574
00:43:16,803 --> 00:43:21,495
<i>Well, he stole and he cheated
And he lied just to survive</i>

575
00:43:22,224 --> 00:43:25,393
<i>With a doodle-dum, diddle-di,
duddle, doodle-dum</i>

576
00:43:27,396 --> 00:43:29,390
<i>With a zippy-zo, zippy-zay,
zippy-zappy-zoopy-zee</i>

577
00:43:30,650 --> 00:43:35,229
<i>Oh, doo-dah, doo-dah, day!</i>

578
00:43:35,363 --> 00:43:37,521
<i>Let me take a little tick
To color in the scene</i>

579
00:43:37,657 --> 00:43:42,283
<i>'Cross the valley lived 3 yokels
Name of Boggis, Bunce and Bean</i>

580
00:43:42,412 --> 00:43:44,617
<i>These 3 crazy jackies
Had our hero on the run</i>

581
00:43:44,746 --> 00:43:46,786
<i>Shot the tail off the cuss
With a fox-shooting gun</i>

582
00:43:46,915 --> 00:43:48,873
<i>But that stylish little fox
Was clever as a whip</i>

583
00:43:49,000 --> 00:43:53,246
<i>Dug as quick as a gopher
That was hyperactive</i>

584
00:43:54,588 --> 00:43:56,748
<i>Now those 3 farmers sit</i>

585
00:43:56,883 --> 00:43:59,089
<i>'Twhere there a hole 'twas once a hill</i>

586
00:43:59,219 --> 00:44:02,801
<i>Singing diddle-dee, daddle-da,
doddle-do, doodle-dum</i>

587
00:44:02,930 --> 00:44:07,509
<i>And as far as I can reckon
They're sitting up there still</i>

588
00:44:07,644 --> 00:44:11,094
<i>Singing zippy-zee, zappy-za, yoppy-yo...</i>

589
00:44:11,229 --> 00:44:13,270
What are you singing, Petey?

590
00:44:14,359 --> 00:44:17,478
Just making it up as I went along, really.

591
00:44:17,778 --> 00:44:22,470
That's just weak songwriting.
You wrote a bad song, Petey!

592
00:44:41,882 --> 00:44:43,259
We took everything!

593
00:44:43,801 --> 00:44:45,425
They took everything?

594
00:44:46,221 --> 00:44:48,345
Let me call you back, Petey.

595
00:44:50,559 --> 00:44:52,847
- They could be anywhere.
- Digging right under our feet.

596
00:44:52,978 --> 00:44:54,555
In a sense,
we've only made matters worse.

597
00:44:54,688 --> 00:44:57,013
We should have stayed out of it.

598
00:45:28,845 --> 00:45:30,423
I've got an idea.

599
00:45:39,481 --> 00:45:41,806
I still don't have a signal.
Is anybody getting reception?

600
00:45:41,941 --> 00:45:44,646
I don't have any signal,
but I've had a problem with that.

601
00:45:44,777 --> 00:45:47,979
Crisp up those ducks! Drag those
chickens! Slow down. We're ahead.

602
00:45:48,113 --> 00:45:51,648
Where are the apples?
Slice them up and get them in the pan.

603
00:45:51,825 --> 00:45:55,824
I can imagine how painful,
even emotionally, that must be for you.

604
00:45:55,955 --> 00:45:57,663
It's not the end of the world.

605
00:45:57,790 --> 00:46:01,408
But how humiliating, having
your whole tail blown clean off by...

606
00:46:01,543 --> 00:46:03,085
Can we drop it?

607
00:46:07,006 --> 00:46:08,716
Yeah, really good, sweet, and nice.

608
00:46:09,218 --> 00:46:11,756
They say you're a natural.
True or false?

609
00:46:12,430 --> 00:46:13,543
Answer the question.

610
00:46:13,680 --> 00:46:15,387
- True, I guess.
- Correct.

611
00:46:15,640 --> 00:46:17,928
Get away, Agnes.
I need a private word with Kristofferson.

612
00:46:18,058 --> 00:46:21,308
- Just a minute. She...
- I don't mind.

613
00:46:21,811 --> 00:46:26,106
I just had a brainstorm
for something fantastic I've got to do.

614
00:46:26,233 --> 00:46:27,479
But I can't do it alone.

615
00:46:27,610 --> 00:46:29,021
- I'm not interested.
- Hear me out.

616
00:46:29,153 --> 00:46:32,901
No, thanks. Foxes from your side
of the family take unnecessary risks.

617
00:46:33,032 --> 00:46:36,779
Only because they've got guts
in their blood. So do we.

618
00:46:37,244 --> 00:46:39,817
- Was I a bit rude to Agnes?
- Yeah.

619
00:46:39,954 --> 00:46:43,289
I should probably say something.
I will in a minute.

620
00:46:44,168 --> 00:46:46,788
- What's the brainstorm?
- In a nutshell?

621
00:46:47,086 --> 00:46:49,458
We're going to steal back my dad's tail.

622
00:46:49,589 --> 00:46:52,791
Whoa! Whew! Hmm...

623
00:47:01,101 --> 00:47:03,222
<i>Okay, chief.
- Here we go.</i>

624
00:47:15,780 --> 00:47:19,908
Well, it took a near catastrophe for
all of you to finally take me up on my offer

625
00:47:20,034 --> 00:47:22,406
to have you over
to the flint-mine for dinner.

626
00:47:22,536 --> 00:47:25,027
Maybe my invitation got lost in the mail.

627
00:47:25,164 --> 00:47:27,736
Does anybody know
what he's talking about?

628
00:47:27,874 --> 00:47:31,458
No, Clive's right.
In all seriousness, excuse me, B.

629
00:47:32,255 --> 00:47:36,298
We do have these 3 ugly farmers
to thank for one thing.

630
00:47:36,424 --> 00:47:39,592
Reminding us to be thankful
and aware of each other.

631
00:47:39,720 --> 00:47:41,878
I'll say it again, aware.

632
00:47:55,902 --> 00:47:58,736
- I don't feel safe.
- That's because we're not.

633
00:47:59,572 --> 00:48:01,529
You should put your bandit hat on.

634
00:48:01,657 --> 00:48:04,907
I don't have one,
but I modified this tube sock.

635
00:48:07,662 --> 00:48:09,786
- We look good.
- Yeah, we do.

636
00:48:10,040 --> 00:48:13,622
Now, where would you keep a prized tail,
if you collected them?

637
00:48:13,751 --> 00:48:17,036
- I'd probably hang it over the mantelpiece.
- Right. Good. In fact...

638
00:48:17,379 --> 00:48:19,289
What's that smell?

639
00:48:22,094 --> 00:48:25,378
Ever tasted one of Mrs. Bean's famous
nutmeg-ginger-apple-snaps?

640
00:48:25,514 --> 00:48:28,265
Well, how do you do?

641
00:48:35,356 --> 00:48:36,850
They are so warm.

642
00:48:40,195 --> 00:48:41,191
Uh-oh.

643
00:48:41,322 --> 00:48:42,648
We got it wrong.

644
00:48:42,779 --> 00:48:44,571
It's not over the mantelpiece.

645
00:48:45,616 --> 00:48:46,895
The necktie.

646
00:49:13,727 --> 00:49:15,433
- Let's go.
- Hang on.

647
00:49:15,560 --> 00:49:16,675
- 2 more.
- She's there.

648
00:49:16,812 --> 00:49:18,305
She can't see.

649
00:49:24,903 --> 00:49:27,988
Look at each other. Here we are. Wow.

650
00:49:28,115 --> 00:49:30,570
I've already had too much to drink
and I'm feeling sentimental

651
00:49:30,700 --> 00:49:32,242
but I'll say something anyway,

652
00:49:32,368 --> 00:49:35,617
which nobody wants to admit,
but which is probably true.

653
00:49:35,747 --> 00:49:38,617
We beat them. We beat those farmers

654
00:49:38,749 --> 00:49:41,156
and now we're triumphantly eating
their roasted chicken,

655
00:49:41,292 --> 00:49:44,378
<i>their sizzling duck, their succulent turkey,
their foie gras...</i>

656
00:49:46,674 --> 00:49:48,417
Where'd the boys go?

657
00:49:49,134 --> 00:49:51,043
- Ash! Kristofferson!
- Boys!

658
00:49:52,429 --> 00:49:54,802
That was crazy.
I can't believe what just happened.

659
00:49:54,932 --> 00:49:57,504
Come on, let's get out of here! Let's go!
Where are we?

660
00:50:01,313 --> 00:50:02,594
Kristofferson?

661
00:50:02,856 --> 00:50:06,983
What am I hearing again, baby?
What's happening? Am I still paranoid?

662
00:50:10,489 --> 00:50:11,769
Cider.

663
00:50:34,553 --> 00:50:38,052
What happened? Something with cider.
That was dangerous. Is anyone hurt?

664
00:50:38,181 --> 00:50:41,431
We're all hurt!
My entire flint-mine got demolished!

665
00:50:41,559 --> 00:50:43,682
Apple juice. Apple juice flood.

666
00:50:43,811 --> 00:50:47,144
Do a head count. Everybody pick a buddy.
Where'd the boys go?

667
00:50:47,272 --> 00:50:49,681
Ash! Kristofferson! Ash!

668
00:50:49,818 --> 00:50:51,016
I'm here!

669
00:50:51,736 --> 00:50:53,692
- Who's your buddy?
- Kristofferson.

670
00:50:53,821 --> 00:50:54,851
- Where is he?
- I don't know.

671
00:50:54,988 --> 00:50:56,020
- Why not?
- I lost him.

672
00:50:56,156 --> 00:50:57,401
You lost him?

673
00:50:57,533 --> 00:51:00,865
We were in the kitchen,
trying to find the necktie.

674
00:51:00,994 --> 00:51:02,404
What are you talking about?

675
00:51:02,537 --> 00:51:03,736
It's my fault.

676
00:51:03,872 --> 00:51:07,206
Where did you get
that nutmeg-ginger-apple-snap,

677
00:51:07,334 --> 00:51:09,706
and why are you wearing
that fake bandit hat?

678
00:51:09,836 --> 00:51:11,378
We went to steal back your tail.

679
00:51:14,340 --> 00:51:16,132
Kristofferson!

680
00:51:24,016 --> 00:51:26,175
Wrap this little mutt in a newspaper

681
00:51:26,310 --> 00:51:28,979
and put him in a box
with some holes punched in the top.

682
00:51:30,064 --> 00:51:32,733
There's one way out of the sewer,
but the manhole cover's closed

683
00:51:32,858 --> 00:51:34,435
and a station wagon's parked on it.

684
00:51:34,567 --> 00:51:37,603
Which means,
we're permanently stuck down here.

685
00:51:38,320 --> 00:51:40,895
You still think we beat them, Foxy?

686
00:51:59,258 --> 00:52:03,303
Badger's right. These farmers
aren't going to quit until they catch me.

687
00:52:03,429 --> 00:52:04,710
I shouldn't have lied to your face

688
00:52:04,847 --> 00:52:09,009
or fallen off the wagon and started
secretly stealing chickens on the sly.

689
00:52:09,141 --> 00:52:13,555
I shouldn't have tried to embarrass
these farmers and cuss with their heads.

690
00:52:13,688 --> 00:52:17,353
I enjoyed it, but I shouldn't have done it.
Now there's only one way out.

691
00:52:17,859 --> 00:52:19,982
If I hand myself over, let them kill me,

692
00:52:20,111 --> 00:52:21,938
stuff me
and hang me over their mantelpiece...

693
00:52:22,072 --> 00:52:26,067
- You'll do no such thing.
- Maybe they'll let everyone else live.

694
00:52:27,741 --> 00:52:31,160
Oh, why did you have to get us
into this, Foxy?

695
00:52:33,082 --> 00:52:36,331
I don't know, but I have a possible theory.

696
00:52:36,459 --> 00:52:39,212
I think I need everyone to think
I'm the greatest,

697
00:52:39,337 --> 00:52:42,255
the "fantastic" Mr. Fox.

698
00:52:42,382 --> 00:52:46,297
If they aren't completely knocked out
and dazzled and intimidated by me

699
00:52:46,428 --> 00:52:48,053
I don't feel good about myself.

700
00:52:48,847 --> 00:52:53,224
Foxes traditionally like to court danger,
hunt prey and outsmart predators.

701
00:52:53,350 --> 00:52:55,179
That's what I'm good at.

702
00:52:56,353 --> 00:52:57,895
At the end of the day, I'm just...

703
00:52:58,021 --> 00:53:00,808
I know. We're wild animals.

704
00:53:03,068 --> 00:53:05,026
I guess we always were.

705
00:53:06,071 --> 00:53:09,854
If I had all this to do over again,
I'd have never let you down.

706
00:53:09,992 --> 00:53:12,662
It was always more fun
when we did it together, anyway.

707
00:53:14,162 --> 00:53:17,530
- I love you, Felicity.
- I love you, too.

708
00:53:19,502 --> 00:53:21,874
But I shouldn't have married you.

709
00:53:31,679 --> 00:53:34,135
I tell you about finding out
we were having a cub?

710
00:53:34,265 --> 00:53:35,677
- In the fox trap.
- Right.

711
00:53:35,809 --> 00:53:37,885
- We were at gunpoint, and your mother...
- Said she's pregnant.

712
00:53:38,019 --> 00:53:39,677
Let me tell it, okay?

713
00:53:39,812 --> 00:53:43,346
I had no idea how to get out of this jam.
Then it hit me.

714
00:53:43,481 --> 00:53:45,308
What do foxes do
better than any other animal?

715
00:53:45,442 --> 00:53:46,852
- Dig.
- You're stepping on my lines.

716
00:53:46,985 --> 00:53:48,692
- Keep telling it.
- So we dug.

717
00:53:48,820 --> 00:53:52,154
And the whole time I put paw over paw,
scooping dirt and pebble,

718
00:53:52,282 --> 00:53:54,025
your mother digging like crazy next to me,

719
00:53:54,159 --> 00:53:56,993
I kept wondering,
who is this little boy going to be?

720
00:53:57,120 --> 00:53:58,234
- Or girl.
- Or girl.

721
00:53:58,370 --> 00:54:00,280
Because at that point, we didn't know.

722
00:54:00,956 --> 00:54:04,075
Ash, I'm so glad he was you.

723
00:54:05,793 --> 00:54:08,002
It's not your fault. It's mine.

724
00:54:30,693 --> 00:54:31,938
Good-bye.

725
00:54:35,906 --> 00:54:40,597
Well, I guess we should probably split
into a certain number of groups

726
00:54:41,495 --> 00:54:43,902
and start doing something, right?

727
00:54:51,296 --> 00:54:53,454
Could I have a glass of water?

728
00:54:56,383 --> 00:54:59,172
Excuse me. Excuse me!

729
00:54:59,347 --> 00:55:03,094
Kristofferson! Hello! Can you hear us?

730
00:55:04,725 --> 00:55:06,054
Kristofferson!

731
00:55:06,185 --> 00:55:07,763
They got the boy.

732
00:55:12,525 --> 00:55:15,230
They want to trade the son for his poppa.

733
00:55:20,700 --> 00:55:23,107
Why did they write this
in letters cut out of magazines?

734
00:55:23,244 --> 00:55:27,571
To protect their identities.
Oh, but then why'd they sign their names?

735
00:55:27,704 --> 00:55:29,993
Plus we already knew who they were
because they're trying to kill us.

736
00:55:30,416 --> 00:55:34,414
"Mr. Fox, we have your son.
If you ever want to see him alive again... "

737
00:55:34,545 --> 00:55:36,419
You took the wrong fox!

738
00:55:39,592 --> 00:55:40,837
I'm his son.

739
00:55:42,679 --> 00:55:44,635
I can see the resemblance.

740
00:55:46,848 --> 00:55:47,846
What was that?

741
00:55:47,977 --> 00:55:49,304
Dad!

742
00:56:07,578 --> 00:56:09,201
Stop, man, stop!

743
00:56:16,418 --> 00:56:18,042
Look at you, girl.

744
00:56:19,087 --> 00:56:21,792
You're still as fine-looking
as a crème brûlée.

745
00:56:23,008 --> 00:56:26,009
Am I being flirted with by a psychotic rat?

746
00:56:33,058 --> 00:56:34,434
Ash!

747
00:56:34,560 --> 00:56:35,839
Let me out!

748
00:56:36,437 --> 00:56:39,226
Excuse me. May I cut in?

749
00:57:12,347 --> 00:57:14,635
The boy's locked in an apple crate

750
00:57:14,766 --> 00:57:19,458
on top of a gun locker
in the attic of Bean Annex.

751
00:57:21,522 --> 00:57:23,561
Would you have told me
if I didn't kill you first?

752
00:57:25,149 --> 00:57:26,182
Never.

753
00:57:27,529 --> 00:57:31,395
All these wasted years.
What were you looking for, Rat?

754
00:57:32,992 --> 00:57:34,901
He's trying to say something, Dad.

755
00:57:36,370 --> 00:57:37,650
Cider.

756
00:57:43,003 --> 00:57:47,692
Here you are, Rat.
A beaker of Bean's finest secret cider.

757
00:57:52,803 --> 00:57:55,471
Like melted gold.

758
00:58:04,313 --> 00:58:08,310
- He redeemed himself.
- Redemption, sure.

759
00:58:09,318 --> 00:58:11,191
But in the end, he's just another dead rat

760
00:58:11,319 --> 00:58:14,273
in a garbage pail
behind a Chinese restaurant.

761
00:58:29,045 --> 00:58:32,581
- He went bananas.
- Yes, he did.

762
00:58:34,259 --> 00:58:35,752
My suicide mission has been canceled.

763
00:58:35,886 --> 00:58:38,292
We're replacing it
with a go-for-broke rescue mission.

764
00:58:40,347 --> 00:58:43,098
In a way,
I'm almost glad that flood interrupted us,

765
00:58:43,225 --> 00:58:46,842
because I don't like the toast I was giving.
I'm going to start over.

766
00:58:50,315 --> 00:58:54,147
When I look down this table,
with the exquisite feast set before us,

767
00:58:54,277 --> 00:58:56,353
I see 2 terrific lawyers,

768
00:58:56,488 --> 00:58:58,479
a skilled pediatrician, a wonderful chef,

769
00:58:58,615 --> 00:59:01,780
a savvy real estate agent,
an excellent tailor, a crack accountant,

770
00:59:01,908 --> 00:59:04,067
a gifted musician,
a pretty good minnow fisherman,

771
00:59:04,202 --> 00:59:08,072
and possibly the best landscape painter
working on the scene today.

772
00:59:08,208 --> 00:59:10,876
Maybe a few of you might even
read my column from time to time.

773
00:59:11,002 --> 00:59:12,496
I tend to doubt it.

774
00:59:13,795 --> 00:59:16,749
I also see a room full of wild animals.

775
00:59:17,632 --> 00:59:21,084
Wild animals with true natures
and pure talents.

776
00:59:21,595 --> 00:59:25,593
With scientific-sounding Latin names
that mean something about our DNA.

777
00:59:26,057 --> 00:59:30,220
Each with his own strengths
and weaknesses due to his or her species.

778
00:59:31,729 --> 00:59:35,892
Anyway, I think it may very well be
all the beautiful differences among us

779
00:59:36,024 --> 00:59:39,525
that might just give us the tiniest glimmer
of a chance of saving my nephew

780
00:59:39,654 --> 00:59:43,865
and letting me make it up to you for
getting us into this crazy whatever-it-is.

781
00:59:43,991 --> 00:59:48,533
I don't know, it's just a thought.
Thank you for listening. Cheers, everyone.

782
00:59:52,123 --> 00:59:53,497
Let's eat!

783
00:59:55,502 --> 00:59:58,041
What? I was just playing along
with the bit he was doing.

784
00:59:59,589 --> 01:00:01,214
Will you join me?

785
01:00:02,466 --> 01:00:03,665
I will.

786
01:00:06,262 --> 01:00:08,717
Oh, all right, I guess.
- Thank you.

787
01:00:09,973 --> 01:00:12,181
Let's start planning.
Who knows shorthand?

788
01:00:12,310 --> 01:00:15,725
<i>Great. Linda, Lutra lutra.
You got some dry paper? Here we go.</i>

789
01:00:15,855 --> 01:00:18,689
<i>- Mole, Talpa europea. What do you got?
- I can see in the dark.</i>

790
01:00:18,815 --> 01:00:20,892
- Incredible. We can use that. Linda?
- Got it.

791
01:00:21,276 --> 01:00:23,233
<i>- Rabbit, Oryctolagus cuniculus.
- I'm fast.</i>

792
01:00:23,362 --> 01:00:24,938
- You bet you are. Linda?
- Got it.

793
01:00:25,195 --> 01:00:26,939
<i>- Beaver, Castor fiber.
- I chew through wood.</i>

794
01:00:27,072 --> 01:00:28,319
- Amazing! Linda!
- Got it.

795
01:00:28,616 --> 01:00:30,823
<i>- Badger, Meles meles!
- Demolitions expert.</i>

796
01:00:30,952 --> 01:00:34,037
- What? Since when?
- Explosions, flames, burning things!

797
01:00:34,164 --> 01:00:36,072
- Demolitions expert. Okay, Linda?
- Got it.

798
01:00:36,373 --> 01:00:39,624
<i>- Weasel, Mustela nivalis!
- Stop yelling!</i>

799
01:00:41,795 --> 01:00:43,621
Ash,
get these little kids organized

800
01:00:43,754 --> 01:00:46,758
and put together a K.P. unit
to keep this sewer clean.

801
01:00:46,885 --> 01:00:47,881
It's good for morale.

802
01:00:48,011 --> 01:00:51,593
- Done. What's K.P.?
- Um, I think it means janitors.

803
01:00:54,850 --> 01:00:58,848
Me. Yo, over here, hey.
I want to go. I want to fight.

804
01:01:00,272 --> 01:01:03,357
<i>Good. Fabulous. Microtus pennsylvanicus.</i>

805
01:01:05,818 --> 01:01:09,483
I didn't get a job yet. Or a Latin name.
What's my strength?

806
01:01:10,115 --> 01:01:12,902
Listen, you're Kylie.
You're an unbelievably nice guy.

807
01:01:13,034 --> 01:01:15,819
Your job is really
just to be available, I think.

808
01:01:15,952 --> 01:01:18,908
I don't know your Latin name. I doubt
they had opossums in ancient Rome.

809
01:01:28,925 --> 01:01:30,916
- It's stupendous! Where's us?
- Right here.

810
01:01:31,051 --> 01:01:32,332
Paint an X.

811
01:01:34,682 --> 01:01:36,555
<i>Dear Farmers Boggis,
Bunce and Bean:</i>

812
01:01:36,683 --> 01:01:39,054
<i>I have no alternative
but to agree to your terms.</i>

813
01:01:39,184 --> 01:01:41,094
<i>Move the station wagon
and open the manhole cover</i>

814
01:01:41,229 --> 01:01:43,470
<i>below the drainpipe by the cobbler shop</i>

815
01:01:43,606 --> 01:01:46,771
<i>and meet me there at 10:00 A.M. sharp.
I will hand myself over to you</i>

816
01:01:46,900 --> 01:01:50,399
<i>in exchange for the boy's safe return.
Cordially, Mr. Fox.</i>

817
01:01:51,614 --> 01:01:54,283
Why'd he write this
in letters cut out of magazines?

818
01:01:54,408 --> 01:01:56,447
I don't know, but you did the same thing.

819
01:01:56,576 --> 01:01:59,743
I don't trust this guy.
Anyway, set up the ambush.

820
01:01:59,996 --> 01:02:03,530
Synchronize your clocks.
The time is now 9:45 A.M.

821
01:02:04,833 --> 01:02:06,825
Put these bandit hats on.

822
01:03:02,222 --> 01:03:03,680
Did you bring the boy?

823
01:03:04,307 --> 01:03:07,592
Of course we did. Say something, kid.

824
01:03:08,811 --> 01:03:11,218
<i>Excuse me. Excuse me!</i>

825
01:03:11,688 --> 01:03:15,058
That doesn't sound anything like him.
It's amateur night in Dixie.

826
01:03:24,243 --> 01:03:26,319
What the cuss is he burning?

827
01:03:27,579 --> 01:03:30,286
<i>Boggis, Bunce, Bean</i>

828
01:03:31,209 --> 01:03:34,125
<i>One fat, one short, one lean</i>

829
01:03:39,840 --> 01:03:41,750
Is that all you've got, Mr. Fox?

830
01:03:43,678 --> 01:03:45,468
<i>Boggis, Bunce and Bean</i>

831
01:03:45,595 --> 01:03:47,340
<i>One fat, one short, one lean</i>

832
01:03:47,473 --> 01:03:49,217
<i>These horrible crooks
So different in looks</i>

833
01:03:49,350 --> 01:03:51,259
<i>Were nonetheless equally mean...</i>

834
01:03:52,061 --> 01:03:53,556
It's unclear whether these...

835
01:03:58,941 --> 01:04:00,732
<i>Boggis, Bunce and Bean</i>

836
01:04:00,860 --> 01:04:02,687
<i>One fat, one short, one lean</i>

837
01:04:02,822 --> 01:04:04,565
<i>Horrible crooks
Different in looks</i>

838
01:04:04,699 --> 01:04:06,573
<i>Nonetheless equally mean</i>

839
01:04:11,580 --> 01:04:13,951
28 pinecones fired, 22 targets hit.

840
01:04:18,002 --> 01:04:19,378
Decoy phase, go.

841
01:04:19,922 --> 01:04:22,294
<i>Yes, sir. Domino Santo,
1, 2, 3.</i>

842
01:04:36,186 --> 01:04:37,266
Dad's on fire!

843
01:04:40,482 --> 01:04:43,566
- Foxy, you're on.
- We're ready.

844
01:04:44,653 --> 01:04:47,487
- I'm going to find him and bring him back.
- I know you will.

845
01:04:48,864 --> 01:04:50,062
Contact!

846
01:05:05,088 --> 01:05:06,749
Are you scared of wolves?

847
01:05:06,882 --> 01:05:08,959
No. I have a phobia of them.

848
01:05:09,093 --> 01:05:12,130
- I have a thing about thunder.
- Why? That's stupid.

849
01:05:12,263 --> 01:05:14,255
I don't like needles myself.

850
01:05:14,390 --> 01:05:16,511
Where'd you come from again?
How did you get in the sidecar?

851
01:05:16,641 --> 01:05:17,970
I feel like I'm losing my mind.

852
01:05:21,519 --> 01:05:24,189
I've got a fox on a motorcycle,
with a littler fox

853
01:05:24,315 --> 01:05:26,807
and what looks like to be
an opossum in the sidecar,

854
01:05:26,943 --> 01:05:28,734
riding north on Farm Lane 7.

855
01:05:29,362 --> 01:05:31,354
Does that sound like anything to anybody?

856
01:05:31,489 --> 01:05:32,900
<i>Red, it's Franklin Bean.</i>

857
01:05:33,032 --> 01:05:36,864
<i>Turn around, get the cuss back here
and pick us up on the ASAP.</i>

858
01:06:03,728 --> 01:06:05,139
Ah!

859
01:06:08,523 --> 01:06:10,481
- You got a credit card?
- Sure.

860
01:06:10,609 --> 01:06:13,692
This is what I was saying about
how good you are, just being available.

861
01:06:13,819 --> 01:06:17,356
A titanium card?
How the cuss did you qualify for this?

862
01:06:17,491 --> 01:06:19,947
I pay my bills on time.
I've always had good credit.

863
01:06:28,208 --> 01:06:29,239
Come on.

864
01:06:29,376 --> 01:06:33,078
What's this thing you do?
The whistle with the clicking sound?

865
01:06:34,132 --> 01:06:35,756
What do you mean? That's my trademark.

866
01:07:07,705 --> 01:07:09,329
Give me a blueberry.

867
01:07:11,084 --> 01:07:12,577
- Blueberry.
- You didn't say anything...

868
01:07:12,710 --> 01:07:16,410
You forgot the blueberries?
I did say it! I wrote it on your paw!

869
01:07:16,840 --> 01:07:18,877
Yeah. It's written on the front of your paw.

870
01:07:22,470 --> 01:07:26,087
- What's that white stuff around his mouth?
- I think he eats soap.

871
01:07:30,894 --> 01:07:33,811
- That's not soap.
- Then why does he have that bubbly...

872
01:07:33,938 --> 01:07:37,603
He's rabid. With rabies.
I've heard about this beagle.

873
01:07:40,029 --> 01:07:42,269
You two go ahead while I distract him.

874
01:07:58,878 --> 01:07:59,828
Hey.

875
01:08:02,173 --> 01:08:03,584
I can fit through there.

876
01:08:05,385 --> 01:08:07,756
- Want to know why?
- Why?

877
01:08:08,595 --> 01:08:10,303
Because I'm little.

878
01:08:11,099 --> 01:08:12,510
Give me that shoelace.

879
01:08:21,401 --> 01:08:22,395
Psst! Psst!

880
01:08:22,527 --> 01:08:24,602
It's me. I'm rescuing you.

881
01:08:26,530 --> 01:08:30,231
- I have mixed feelings about that.
- I don't blame you.

882
01:08:33,912 --> 01:08:36,829
Can you give me a karate lesson
real quick?

883
01:08:37,875 --> 01:08:40,329
Okay. Stand like this.

884
01:08:40,834 --> 01:08:42,578
Position yourself on the balls of your feet.

885
01:08:42,711 --> 01:08:45,796
Close your eyes.
You weigh less than a slice of bread.

886
01:08:48,217 --> 01:08:51,003
I feel there's a tenderness in your eyes,
isn't there?

887
01:08:51,387 --> 01:08:52,667
Yes, I'm right.

888
01:08:53,221 --> 01:08:57,682
Let's review the principal agility
techniques: jumping, flipping, landing.

889
01:08:58,017 --> 01:09:02,266
You're a good boy.
A little lonely, maybe, but terribly sweet.

890
01:09:02,774 --> 01:09:05,808
Is your name Spitz?
That's German, isn't it?

891
01:09:06,692 --> 01:09:09,314
Now, a rudimentary version
of the cyclone chop.

892
01:09:09,446 --> 01:09:12,019
First, get a running start,
which, obviously, I can't do here.

893
01:09:12,157 --> 01:09:13,983
Then, at the destination of the chop,

894
01:09:14,116 --> 01:09:16,952
lean and thrust into the point of contact,
paw open and straight,

895
01:09:17,079 --> 01:09:19,700
then withdraw instantaneously.
It's the pull-back that matters.

896
01:09:19,831 --> 01:09:22,500
The pull-back generates
the force of the impact.

897
01:09:23,083 --> 01:09:24,282
Got it.

898
01:09:25,837 --> 01:09:29,039
Yeah, I'm just going to chop this thing
right off.

899
01:09:38,432 --> 01:09:39,842
He's going to do it.

900
01:09:42,769 --> 01:09:45,888
Why, you're just as sweet as a...

901
01:09:47,314 --> 01:09:49,769
I thought he said
never look a beagle in the eye.

902
01:10:00,745 --> 01:10:03,698
- Did you chop it?
- Oh, no. Oh, no.

903
01:10:18,011 --> 01:10:19,504
Kristofferson.

904
01:10:20,971 --> 01:10:22,595
Uh, I'm okay.

905
01:10:23,891 --> 01:10:26,513
- I'm okay.
- I'm sorry.

906
01:10:27,311 --> 01:10:30,348
That's all right. You were just trying
to unlock the apple crate.

907
01:10:31,190 --> 01:10:33,811
No. I mean I'm sorry about...

908
01:10:34,735 --> 01:10:38,482
Oh, you mean from before. The apology
you owed me, but never actually said.

909
01:10:38,613 --> 01:10:43,276
Right. I'm grumpy. I spit.
I wake up on the wrong side of the bed.

910
01:10:43,410 --> 01:10:46,945
I'm just different, apparently.

911
01:10:47,080 --> 01:10:49,286
But it won't happen again.

912
01:10:50,667 --> 01:10:52,291
Kristofferson, I'm sorry.

913
01:10:54,920 --> 01:10:56,961
Well, that's all right, too.

914
01:10:57,675 --> 01:10:58,920
Throw me the shoelace, please.

915
01:11:10,726 --> 01:11:12,185
You okay?

916
01:11:44,426 --> 01:11:45,707
He's wearing it.

917
01:11:49,890 --> 01:11:54,582
Your tractors uprooted my tree,
your posse hunted my family,

918
01:11:54,895 --> 01:11:59,585
your gunmen kidnapped my nephew,
your rat insulted my wife,

919
01:12:00,231 --> 01:12:02,558
and you shot off my tail.

920
01:12:02,735 --> 01:12:05,772
I'm not leaving here without that necktie.

921
01:12:07,157 --> 01:12:08,437
Kill him!

922
01:12:17,248 --> 01:12:19,623
Actually, we should just go.
Where did I park?

923
01:12:20,252 --> 01:12:22,210
I weigh less than a slice of bread.

924
01:12:23,172 --> 01:12:25,163
- I'll be right back.
- Ash!

925
01:12:33,973 --> 01:12:37,425
Dodge the grabbers, duck the taggers,
jump the twig-basket and

926
01:12:37,560 --> 01:12:39,221
knock the cedar-stick off the cross-rock!

927
01:12:58,372 --> 01:12:59,699
Hot box!

928
01:13:29,026 --> 01:13:33,106
Ash, that was pure wild animal craziness.
You're an athlete.

929
01:13:33,822 --> 01:13:34,817
Mmm-hmm.

930
01:13:34,948 --> 01:13:37,105
Here, put this bandit hat on.

931
01:13:40,327 --> 01:13:41,609
Goggles.

932
01:13:42,497 --> 01:13:43,908
<i>Boggis, Bunce and Bean</i>

933
01:13:44,040 --> 01:13:45,368
<i>One fat, one short
one lean</i>

934
01:13:51,589 --> 01:13:52,620
Are you going to...

935
01:13:55,299 --> 01:13:59,880
Holy swearing cuss!

936
01:14:10,357 --> 01:14:12,064
Petey, bring us a ladder, please.

937
01:14:13,109 --> 01:14:14,306
Stand by!

938
01:14:14,445 --> 01:14:17,280
I just intercepted
a high-frequency radio signal with the can,

939
01:14:17,406 --> 01:14:18,864
they're on their way home!

940
01:14:28,122 --> 01:14:30,577
Don't turn around.

941
